body:
The hall is full again.

The families sit in their rows in their good clothes, as they have sat since the afternoon of Commencement, every one of them caught between one breath and the next. The graduating class stands along the wall in borrowed robes: twenty-six of them, and a gap. The Keepers stand in the east doors in sealed-sky blue, stopped mid-step. The spell hangs where it has always hung, a line of white fire an arm's length from the place where you stood. {sphere_state}

In among all of it, moving, are the people you brought through the edge: everyone who would come, and everyone who had to. They stand in the aisles between the families the way you stand in someone else's house, and they keep their voices down.

{dais_wenna}

{dais_aurel}

{dais_hess}

{dais_joren}

{dais_namer}

{dais_conductor}

{dais_engineer}

{dais_traitor}

The Astrologer's chair is empty. It was empty at the naming, and it is empty now, and everyone in the hall who is alive keeps not looking at it.

You go up to the lectern, where the Provost stands with her mouth open on the next word, and take your place beside her. Above the place where the Register lay, the ink of the next letter still stands in the air, unfinished. It is the one thing in the hall that is yours. It is what he is coming to finish.

{remembrance}

'Be it recorded,' says the Register, 'that the hall is full.' It sounds, for once, as if it would like to stop there.

Before you open the door, you ask them what to do with it. It is the last thing you will be able to ask anyone.

{council_votes}

{council_tally}

'The answer,' says the Register, 'is the candidate's. The Register has looked for a precedent. There is none.'

At the sphere, {traitor_full} watches the Keepers come up the aisle.

{hold_glass}

'{traitor_redemption}'

{penance_kept}

The Keepers see who is standing at the sphere, and how. You watch them see it. For the first time all night, they hesitate.

The one who pledged to you comes in with the Keepers.

{walk_in}

{seen_coming}

{at_sphere}

'{traitor_second}'

{treachery_kept}

The glass, which has been breaking for a year, begins to break again.

Up the aisle in the middle of the Keepers comes {traitor_full}, walking the way the Keepers walk: as if every step had been read in advance.

{lost_to_him}

'{traitor_second}'

{voice_words}

The last Keeper in the hall goes down on the examination floor, and after that there is only the rain at the doors, hanging.

{front_news}

{seventh_layer}

Above the lectern the letter of ink still stands in the air. It held.

'Be it recorded,' says the Register, 'that the hall held.' A pause. 'Again.'

The wound in the sphere is right there.

Inside the Second it is still the instant it happened: six shards a finger's width apart, and between them, where the Seal was, a breach no wider than a hand, fresh, still opening. Through it, very faintly, comes the sound of something enormous turning over in its sleep.

{rebinding_pieces}

{rebinding_begin}

He comes in by the east doors, out of the rain.

He is ordinary. You knew that from the glass, and it is still a surprise: a man of middle years in a traveling coat, wet to the shoulders from the running world's weather, with ink on the side of one finger. He does not hurry. He walks up the aisle between the families the way a man walks into a library he has heard about all his life, reading the spines.

At the foot of the dais he stops, and looks for a while at his own chair, which is empty. Then he looks up at the ceiling.

'So this is where the sentence hides,' says Kassian Vor-Aleth. 'Do you know, I have read every sky but this ceiling. Let us finish the passage together, and see which of us was the author.'

{hess_watch}

He looks around the hall once more, slowly, the way you look for one face in a crowd. 'He is not here. The one who wrote this. I thought he might be.' He does not say what he would have said to him. You have the strong impression he has been composing it for a year.

Then he looks at you.

{first_sight}

{study_line}

{deal_line}

{path_read}

The breach closes.

It does not slam. It closes the way a wound closes, from the edges in, and the last sound from the other side goes with it.

{rebound_pieces}

Out in the running world the wounds stop leaking. Over the next new moon the blight around them dies back, and the things that came up through the seep go quiet, or go home. In a farm village a child who lit a candle last winter, with nobody to teach her, tries again and cannot. Nobody will ever know whether that was a mercy.

The Fast will ring its bells for the dead and say that the fast has been kept. Wenna Aldric's entrance examination will go on getting easier. Somewhere at the end of a passage there is a room with a lamp in it. You have bet the world that someone will be there to take it.

{warden_held}

{fates_open}

{companion_fates}

{honored_spirits}

The Register writes every one of them down in their own hands. When it reaches the end of the list it leaves one line empty, the way you leave a chair.

The glass takes the pieces and holds, and asks for one thing more.

You knew it would. Everyone in the hall knew. The Seal was never only six refusals: when it was forged, a seventh school gave itself to keep it, and the land counted seven. Tonight the glass needs someone to stay in it, and hold the places that would otherwise come apart.

{anchor_volunteer}

{anchor_after}

{conferral_setting}

{conferral_place}

{conferral_speaker}

{conferral_words}

{conferral_hand}

The name, at last, is {wizard_name}, of {guild_name}. Not a school. Any faculty in the world could have named you into a school, and some of them did. The other half was never theirs to give.

At the foot of the dais the Examiner speaks. Her nod finished hours ago. Now the board has had its say, and she keeps her word, and tells you what she thinks. {examiner_opinion} {examiner_margin}

The Register writes it in her hand. It is a complete sentence. So is the next one.

{erratum_margin}

'Be it recorded,' says the Register, 'that the candidate was named.' Then it stops, because the sentence is finished. It is the first sentence it has finished since Commencement, and it reads it over to itself, twice, before it turns the page.

Afterward, the Register reads you the minutes from the beginning. It is a long time since anyone asked it to.

{reading_recap}

'The minutes are complete,' it says at last, and closes, and for once it does not begin another sentence.

{defeat_where}

The anchor takes you back before the blow lands. The timeline closes over the Convergence like water. He does not gloat; he schedules. Somewhere behind your eyes, the Long Second holds, and everything carried inside it holds with you.

'Be it recorded,' the Register says, much later, on the campus side of the edge, 'that the door was opened. Once.' It does not say anything else for a long time.
